  "final_text": "All four steps are done in Kibana 9.5.5, and the rule reads back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (id `d3d7af60-4c81-11e8-b3d7-01146121b73d`, index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has 33 fields. Its time field is `timestamp`. `Carrier`, `Cancelled` and `timestamp` are the fields used below.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-e982d32c Flights' (id `fc8d0160-af29-4915-9616-7e79fc0e35e1`) has exactly two Lens panels, both on the data view by reference:\n   - a line chart of flight count over time, using a date histogram on `timestamp`;\n   - a bar chart of flight count by carrier, using terms on `Carrier`. It was first titled \"Number of flights per carrier\".\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-e982d32c Cancelled flights' (id `ecd2ebde-a2bc-47f9-a41a-e80e868900a7`) is an Elasticsearch query rule, created enabled, so no separate enable call was needed. It runs every 1m, filters with the KQL query `Cancelled : true` on the data view, and fires when the count over the last 5m is greater than 10. It has no actions attached.\n4. **Panel edit:** The carrier panel is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. I read the dashboard back and it has two panels with the expected titles.\n\nI read the rule back after the panel edit, and `enabled` is `True`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
